---
const ties=[202,201,103];
---
<figure class="pgn-figure" aria-labelledby="ties-title">
  <figcaption id="ties-title"><span class="pgn-number">Figure 2</span>Continue inside the timestamp tie</figcaption>
  <div class="pgn-scene">
    <p class="pgn-key">Page 1 ended at (20 Oct 10:15 UTC, 201).</p>
    <div class="pgn-columns">
      <div><p class="pgn-panel-label">Time alone · placed_at &lt; cursor time</p>{ties.map(id=><div class="pgn-tie-row"><strong>{id}</strong><span>20 Oct · 10:15<small>Equal time → excluded</small></span></div>)}<p class="pgn-result">Next page: <strong>102, 101</strong></p></div>
      <div><p class="pgn-panel-label">Whole key · (placed_at, order_id) &lt; cursor</p>{ties.map(id=><><div class="pgn-tie-row" data-answer={id<201?'yes':'no'}><strong>{id}</strong><span>20 Oct · 10:15<small>{id>201?'ID is greater → before the boundary':id===201?'The boundary itself → excluded':'ID is smaller → keep this row'}</small></span></div>{id===201&&<p class="pgn-boundary">↓ continue after 201</p>}</>)}<p class="pgn-result">Next page: <strong>103, 102</strong></p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="pgn-caption">Descending time and descending ID. The time-only test loses 103. Comparing the pair keeps it; the strict comparison excludes the boundary row 201.</p>
</figure>
